# `@vltpkg/security-archive`

The types here are the foundation for giving vlt users a transparent
view of vulnerabilities, malware, typosquats, and supply-chain risks,
with actionable remediation. A type change that drops a field, closes
a union, or makes an optional value required can silently hide
findings from every consumer. Every invariant below exists to keep
that transparency intact — don't relax one without tracing every
consumer (`vlt audit`, `vlt query` security footer, `:severity()`).

Command-level behaviour is in `@audit.mdc`.

## Type invariants (`src/security-archive/src/types.ts`)

- **`AlertType` is an open union.** The `(string & {})` fallback is
  load-bearing — the feed adds types faster than this union tracks
  them. Closing it would make an unrecognized alert fail to typecheck,
  hiding a novel risk from every consumer. Never close it.
- **`AlertCategory` is exactly six values**: `supplyChainRisk`,
  `quality`, `maintenance`, `vulnerability`, `license`, `other`.
  `malware`, `severity`, `squat` are alert *types*, not categories.
  **Never classify on `category` alone** — malware and typosquats are
  both `supplyChainRisk` alongside every capability finding. Match on
  `alert.type`, or malware counts as zero while detail rows say
  "malware: critical".
- **`AlertSeverity` excludes `medium`.** Wire spelling is `middle`.
  Never add `medium` — the exclusion forces consumers to map it
  rather than compare directly, which is the only way a `medium`
  finding stays visible instead of falling through to `low`.
- **`PackageAlert.severity` and `.category` are optional.** When
  severity is absent or unrecognized, don't default to `low`: treat
  malware as `critical`, else `low`, emit `warn()`. Defaulting to `low`
  is silent data loss — a malware alert spelled `moderate` would be
  filed `low`, dropped by `--audit-level=high`, and the user sees exit
  `0` (see `@audit.mdc`).
- **`AlertAction` — always respect `ignore`.** Filter at the single
  point alerts enter (`getPackageAlerts`), not at display. Otherwise
  bucketing, counts, and rendered rows disagree about which risks
  exist. A package whose every alert is ignored drops out entirely.
- **`AlertFix` presence is authoritative** — `fix` present ⇒ fixable,
  absent ⇒ not fixable. No registry resolution needed. `fix.description`
  is Socket's own CLI prose — never render it; remediation must come
  from vlt's own data, not redirect to another tool.
- **`isVulnerabilityAlert`** narrows to `VulnerabilityAlertType`. Use
  it before reading CVE-specific `props` (`cveId`, `cwes`, `title`,
  `cvss`, `vulnerableVersionRange`) — reading those on a non-vuln
  alert would fabricate advisory detail that isn't there.

## Synthetic alerts

`vlt query`'s footer aggregates **without** a security archive, so the
synthetic-alert fallback in `getPackageAlerts` is load-bearing, not
dead code. Synthetic alerts must emit `middle` via `toWireSeverity` —
never `medium` (a value the endpoint can't produce). Without this
fallback, `vlt query` with security selectors would show zero findings
even when insights flag malware or CVEs.

## Schema fidelity

Check <https://docs.socket.dev/reference/batchpackagefetch> before
adding or changing a field. `props.epss` and `reachability` are
declared but unused — keep them so the wire shape stays accurate;
don't render them as if they were scored.

## Breaking-change discipline

Published (not private) — exported type changes are breaking. Silent
breaks are the danger: `boolean` → `LeveledInsights` still compiles
`if (insights.malware)` but always sees truthy. Bump version, update
consumers (`@vltpkg/query`, `@vltpkg/cli-sdk`, `:severity()`), call
out breaks in the PR body.

## Runtime (`src/security-archive/src/index.ts`)

- `SecurityArchive` extends `LRUCache<DepID, PackageReportData>`
- Uses Socket API v0 (`https://api.socket.dev/v0/purl?alerts=true`) with public API token
- Stores in SQLite DB (XDG data dir) with TTL-based expiration
- Only public npm registry nodes are queued for the Socket API
  (`splitDepID` → `registry`, resolves to `https://registry.npmjs.org/`
  or the URL configured as `registries.npm` via `usesNpmRegistry`).
  Other registries are skipped. `vlt login --registry=<mirror>` writes
  only the scalar `registry` option, so a non-npmjs.org mirror
  configured that way is not scanned — use `vlt setup` or
  `vlt config set registries=npm=<url>`.
- Stale-while-revalidate via **detached child process**
  (`update-expired.ts`) — don't make it sync; `vlt ls`/`vlt query`
  must not block on revalidation
- `browser.ts` is a plain `Map` from JSON dump — shares `types.ts`
  with the Node build; don't diverge them
